            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for to shake one's head, then the Mandarin for bodily pain."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: xīnzàng",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "xīnzàng — \"the heart as an organ\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ yéye de xīnzàng bú tài hǎo — My grandfather has a weak heart."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "xīnzàng is built from 心 'heart' and zàng 'an inner organ'. The first is inherited from Middle Chinese sim (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), glossed as the organ of fire; no dictionary we use records an older form of the second under this spelling, so its history is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying how it was."
            }
          ]
        },
